 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed event revisions are pushed to connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets, backed by replayable revision history for reconnect catch-up. Five-second HTTPS polling (simpler stateless operation, but repeated reads and multi-second lag) and a hosted realtime service (less connection management, but recurring vendor fees and provider coupling) were rejected to get fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor, accepting the operational work of managing connection capacity and reconnects. See `docs/decisions/change-delivery.md`.
